WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.320
Follow LangFocus on Facebook, Twitter and Instagram.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.400
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel
 and my name is Paul.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.600
Today's video is the third installment
 in the Mystery Languages series.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.900
Maybe you've seen Mystery Languages one and two. 
If not, you can find it right here.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.000
But here is how it works:

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.280
I'll play you a sample of the language, and then,
I'll show you a written sample of the language

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.420
and you can make a educated guess 
as to what that language is.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.640
And don't worry if you can't guess 
the exact language correctly.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.240
The important thing is that you tell us your guesses 
and your reasons for your guesses 
in the comments down below.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.320
And that will start a lot of interesting discussions.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.360
So let's get right into the first language.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.060
Please listen.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.360
Now let's see some written samples of that language.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.800
This means : "How much is this?"

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.220
This means : "Where is the toilet?"

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.800
This means : "Speak more slowly, please"

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.320
This means : "Could you write it please?"

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.180
So, what language is it?

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.340
The language is Catalan.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.260
A Romance language spoken mostly 
in eastern Spain as well as in Andorra.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.420
And in part of France

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.060
If you guessed Spanish or Portuguese or another 
Romance language, then you're not too far off.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.220
In fact, Catalan has 85% lexical similarity with Spanish

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.880
But the two are only partly mutually intelligible.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.660
Catalan is part of the Occitano-Romance subfamily,
along with Occitan.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.560
It's always interesting to take a look at a Romance 
language that you're not too familiar with,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.220
because you might see some features 
of one Romance language

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.520
and some different features 
from another Romance language.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.700
If we look at this sentence again,
"podria" looks like the spanish word for "could"

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:59.100
This word "escriure" seems kind of like "écrire" in French 
or "scrivere" in Italian which means "to write".

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.380
And this phrase "si us plau" seems like 
"s'il vous plaît" in French

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:09.540
So you can see why some people say that learning 
two Romance languages helps you understand 
the fair bit of the others. At least in writing.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.360
Okay, on to the next language.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:12.320
Please listen

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.340
And again let's have a look at some written samples.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.020
This means "how are you?" when said to a male.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.480
This means "Pleased to meet you".

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.000
This means "what is your name?" when said to a male.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.660
This means "where are you from?"

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.080
So what language is it?

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.380
The language's Amharic.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.460
Amharic is a language spoken in Ethiopia
by around 22 million people.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.040
It's a member of the Semitic language family
meaning that it's related to Arabic and Hebrew.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.440
But it's not intelligible with those languages.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.400
I've studied Hebrew and Arabic in the past
but I can't understand Amharic at all.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.400
But there are a couple of cognates 
I can see in these written samples.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.620
Like the word for "name" : "sem".

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.340
In Arabic the word is "ism".

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.820
And in Hebrew, it's "shem".

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.060
Here in Amharic "semeh" is your name

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.140
with the suffix "eh" being a possessive pronoun.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.680
In Arabic, you'd say "ismak" 
and in Hebrew, you'd say "shimcha"

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.780
Also "man" is the word for "what",

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.500
which might be related to the Arabic word "maa" 
and the Hebrew word "ma".

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.400
Okay. On to the next language.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.260
Please listen.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.720
And again let's see some written samples
of this language.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.720
This means "Please to meet you".

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.260
This means "Where are you from?"

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.980
This means "Long time no see".

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.640
This means "Have a good journey"

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:15.160
So what language is it?

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.460
It's the Tamil language.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.020
Tamil is a language spoken in southern India 
as well as in Sri Lanka,

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.220
as well as by Tamil communities overseas 
in places like Malaysia and Singapore.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.460
Tamil is a Dravidian language which is unrelated to
the Indo-European languages spoken in northern India.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:36.120
Tamil has a long literary history reaching back 
to around 2,600 years ago.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:42.700
The Tamil script is an abugida meaning a script 
in which consonants and vowels are written together 
as units which can't be separated.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.160
Come to think of it, the Amharic script that 
we had a glance at earlier is also an abugida.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.920
Well, I hope you had fun guessing 
today's mystery languages.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.120
Be sure to leave your guesses and your reasons 
for your guesses in the comments down below.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.380
And I'm sure that will start 
lots of interesting discussions.

